From a6d4190a40feabd758335a0f7ee2e8c49443900e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: amd-weisun <Wei.Sun@amd.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:11:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Serve the FP8 paged-attention decode from aiter on gfx950
 where it is faster

paged_attention_decode_fp8 now picks per shape between MSLK's FlyDSL kernel
and aiter's FlyDSL pa_decode, which takes the op's cache layout, scales and
block tables as they are. aiter's ASM pa_fwd_asm is not a candidate: its
softmax scale is fixed to 1/sqrt(128), it takes only block size 16, and it was
slower than both.

- mslk/attention/aiter/paged_attention_decode_fp8.py: builds aiter's work plan
  on the GPU and calls pa_decode; aiter uses its own scratch.
- mslk/attention/dispatch/paged_attention_decode_fp8.py: the backends, which
  calls aiter takes (any call aiter would reject, an empty batch, or one the
  table was not tuned for, such as f16 queries, stays on MSLK's kernel), and the table key: heads, head size, block size and the
  context the block table holds (rounded up to a power of two), with the batch
  size as the bucket. All come from shapes, so a CUDA graph replays the backend
  it was captured with.
- bench/aiter/tune_paged_attention_decode_fp8.py and its shape list. The key
  is the block table's capacity, but callers often size tables for longer
  contexts than they hold, so each shape is tuned twice through the shared
  driver: with lengths filling the table and with lengths up to 1/16 of it.
  A shape goes to aiter if aiter was correct in both runs and its total time
  is lower (requiring it to win both would drop most long-context wins: its
  ~12 us per-call floor loses short runs at small batches). The runs' tables
  and logs are in bench/aiter/tuned/; the committed table in
  mslk/attention/dispatch/tables/ records both runs' timings. aiter serves
  296 of 1008 rows.
- Tests: the op's tests run through the routing and with each backend forced,
  plus shapes the table sends to each backend and calls aiter cannot take;
  test/attention/dispatch_test.py and the lazy-import test gain the op.
  package_data, the ROCm CI path filter and the CI test list gain the files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
